From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AxxzyWasTaken <axxzytte@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 11:17:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: clpc: scale the T6040/T6041 GPU floor
 with its busy share

On T6040 and T6041 any reported GPU work holds the top memory and fabric
floor. At 120 Hz a scrolling browser keeps the GPU reported busy most of
the time for a few percent of real execution, so light desktop use pays
for the top DCS state.

Let the GPU driver report its busy share per window with
apple_clpc_gpu_set_busy() and pick the floor from a per-SoC table in
struct apple_clpc_hw: on T6040/T6041 DCS 1, 2, 4 or 5 and fabric 0, 0, 0
or 2, split at gpu_busy_mid (160), gpu_busy_high (500) and gpu_busy_top
(800) permille. A higher level applies at once. A lower one applies at
the first CLPC sample at least 400 ms after the last sample that asked
for the current level or a higher one, so the down-step happens at least
400 ms after the last change, later if the sampler runs late. A GPU that
reports no share, and every SoC without the table, keeps the top of the
map. The debugfs status shows the reported share and the current level
without moving it.

The CPU side had the same problem: a cluster at its own top speed asked
for DCS 5 and fabric 2 even when that was the E cluster. Take the map
quarters from 4.512 GHz for both clusters, and lower the T6040 map to
DCS 1, 1, 3, 4 with fabric 0. On the M4 Max on AC, compared with the old
table: scrolling 24.9 W to 22.1 W at 120 fps with no long frames, a page
workload 23.84 W to 22.73 W for 0.9 % less score.

The CLPC host demand test now covers the busy-share table, a GPU that
reports no share and the down-step hold.

Signed-off-by: AxxzyWasTaken <axxzytte@gmail.com>
(cherry picked from commit d4607f83dce00c2ac04daf4f7a4d1d3b3e8db7a8)
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
